      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given the quadratic equation $x^2 - 63x + k = 0$, let's denote the roots by $p$ and $q$. From Vieta's formulas, we know:\n1. $p + q = 63$—(Equation 1)\n2. $pq = k$—(Equation 2)\n\nSince $p$ and $q$ are prime, we can assume $p$ and $q$ are different primes. The first step is to find a combination of two prime numbers that adds up to 63. We find that the pairs of prime numbers that add up to 63 are:\n- (2, 61)\n- (3, 60) — However, 60 is not a prime number\n- (5, 58) — But 58 is not a prime number\n- (11, 52) — However, 52 is not a prime number\n- (13, 50) — Again, 50 is not a prime number\n- (17, 46) — This also does not work as some of the numbers are not prime\n- (19, 44) — 44 is not a prime number\n- (23, 40) — 40 is not a prime number\n- (29, 34) — None of these are prime\n- (31, 32) — None of these are prime\n\nFrom the above analysis, we are left with only one pair of prime numbers: (29, 34), but 34 is not a prime number.\n\nTherefore, the only valid pair of prime numbers that sum up to 63 is (29, 34), but as the question requires two different prime numbers, there are no valid pairs.\n\nThus, the number of possible values of $k$ is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).",
