      "text": "Let's denote the two prime roots by $q$ and $r$, so that the equation becomes $(x-q)(x-r)=x^2-(q+r)x+qr$. This means that the coefficients of the two polynomials must be equal. In our case, the constant terms are equal, and the coefficients of the linear terms are also equal. Thus, $q+r=63$ and $qr=k$. It is readily verified through trial and error that \\begin{align*} \\text{For }(q=2,\\ r=61) \\implies k&=2 \\cdot 61 \\implies 122, \\\\ \\text{For }(q=3,\\ r=60) \\implies k &= 3 \\cdot 60 = 180, \\\\ \\text{For }(q=5,\\ r=58) \\implies k=5 \\cdot 58 = 290. \\end{align*} Note that the roots remain the same when $(q, r)$ and $(\\text{Prime}(q), \\text{Prime}(r))$ are exchanged. Thus, if $(q, r)$ satisfies $qr = k$, then $(r, q)$ also satisfies this relation, and $k$ is only obtained twice. Hence, there are $\\boxed{3}$ possible values of $k$. The answer is: 3",
